"{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.2307/j.ctvr43jmf.14\",\n  \"title\": \"Proto-Dravidian and the Indus Script\",\n  \"authors\": \"\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.2307/j.ctvr43jmf.14\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2015-02-06T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvr43jmf.14\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"book-chapter\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Archaeopress Publishing Ltd', 'container_title': 'The Archaeology and Epigraphy of Indus Writing', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '77-99', 'issn': [], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'book-chapter', 'member': '1121', 'prefix': '10.2307'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.31235/osf.io/ftdzc\",\n  \"title\": \"Can the semasiographic/logographic Indus script answer the Dravidian question? Insights from Indus script’s gemstone related fish-signs, and Indus gemstone-word “maṇi”\",\n  \"authors\": \"Bahata Ansumali Mukhopadhyay\",\n  \"abstract\": \"<p>From archaeogenetic, linguistic, archaeological, and ethnographic evidences, several scholars have linked the people of ancient Indus valley civilization (IVC) with the ancestors of Dravidian speakers. This article argues that the semasiographically/logographically written Indus script (ISC) used certain fish-like logograms, whose iconicity emerged from certain Indus symbolisms, which are linkable with certain linguistic symbolisms found across today’s Dravidian languages. As discussed in the author’s previous articles, ISC was a mercantile script, and different segments of the formulaic ISC inscriptions were usually populated by signs belonging to specific semantic classes.  The core-informational segment, which occur preceding the phrase-final and pre-phrase-final signs ( ), arguably encoded names of licensed crafts, and taxed commodities (e.g., the crucible-blowpipe based sign   which signified precious-metals/gold-smithy). This article claims that the fish-like ISC signs ( , , , , , ,  ), which mainly occur in the ‘core-informational’ segments, often accompanying the gold/precious-metal sign   (e.g.,  ,  ), possibly encoded meanings related to gemstones, lapidary activities, related precious commodities, barter equivalencies, and taxes/licenses. Interestingly, in various Indus settlements, seals/tablets containing fish-sign- inscriptions are found in unusually high concentration in the archaeological contexts of lapidary workshops and jeweler’s shops. Revealingly, the polysemic Proto-Dravidian fish-word “mīn”, a popular fish-word used across present India, also signifies ‘shining’, ‘bright’, and ‘gemstone’ in several Dravidian languages, and is etymologically connected to the Proto-Dravidian root-verb “*mīn”, which signifies “to shine,” “to glitter,” etc. In the Indian subcontinent, this fish-based homonymy for gemstones and related linguistic symbolism for other glittering things (“*min”-based words coined for stars, fireflies, lightning, etc.) are found only in the Dravidian language-group, indicating that the gemstone related fish-sign symbolism of ISC might have emerged from an ancestral Dravidian culture prevalent in IVC.  Relevantly, in Mesopotamia, the eye-patterned apotropaic gemstone-beads imported from IVC were called “fish-eye” stones (“NA4-IGI- ḪA”, “NA4-IGI-KU6”), and \\\"eye-stones\\\" of Meluhha (\\\"NA4-IGI-ME-LUḪ-ḪA\\\"), whereas in Dravidian languages, carnelian (“akki-kal”), tiger-eye-like sardonyx (“puli-k-kaṇ-kal”), and cat’s-eye-like chrysoberyl (“pūṉai-k-kaṇ-kal”) have eye-stone related epithets. Moreover, “maṇi”, the Indus word for gemstones, which remained fossilized in ancient documents of Near East, arguably has a Dravidian root. Intriguingly, “maṇi” has been signifying meanings like “amulet” (see Atharvaveda), “bead”, “apple of eye”, and “gemstone” in Indian languages, each which were linked to the amuletic, eye-patterned gemstone beads of Meluhah.</p>\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.31235/osf.io/ftdzc\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2023-04-08T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.31235/osf.io/ftdzc\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"posted-content\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Center for Open Science', 'container_title': '', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '', 'issn': [], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'posted-content', 'member': '15934', 'prefix': '10.31235'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.2139/ssrn.4412558\",\n  \"title\": \"Can the Semasiographic/logographic Indus Script Answer the Dravidian Question? Insights from Indus Script's Gemstone Related Fish-Signs, and Indus Gemstone-Word 'maṇi'\",\n  \"authors\": \"Bahata A. Mukhopadhyay\",\n  \"abstract\": \"<jats:p>From archaeogenetic, linguistic, archaeological, and ethnographic evidences, several scholars have linked the people of ancient Indus valley civilization (IVC) with the ancestors of Dravidian speakers. This article argues that the semasiographically/logographically written Indus script (ISC) used certain fish-like logograms, whose iconicity emerged from certain Indus symbolisms, which are linkable with certain linguistic symbolisms found across today’s Dravidian languages. As discussed in the author’s previous articles, ISC was a mercantile script, and different segments of the formulaic ISC inscriptions were usually populated by signs belonging to specific semantic classes. The core-informational segment, which occur preceding the phrase-final and pre-phrase-final signs ( ), arguably encoded names of licensed crafts, and taxed commodities (e.g., the crucible-blowpipe based sign which signified precious-metals/gold-smithy). This article claims that the fish-like ISC signs which mainly occur in the ‘core-informational’ segments, often accompanying the gold/precious-metal sign possibly encoded meanings related to gemstones, lapidary activities, related precious commodities, barter equivalencies, and taxes/licenses. Interestingly, in various Indus settlements, seals/tablets containing fish-sign- inscriptions are found in unusually high concentration in the archaeological contexts of lapidary workshops and jeweler’s shops. Revealingly, the polysemic Proto-Dravidian fish-word “mīn”, a popular fish-word used across present India, also signifies ‘shining’, ‘bright’, and ‘gemstone’ in several Dravidian languages, and is etymologically connected to the Proto-Dravidian root-verb “*mīn”, which signifies “to shine,” “to glitter,” etc. In the Indian subcontinent, this fish-based homonymy for gemstones and related linguistic symbolism for other glittering things (“*min”-based words coined for stars, fireflies, lightning, etc.) are found only in the Dravidian language-group, indicating that the gemstone related fish-sign symbolism of ISC might have emerged from an ancestral Dravidian culture prevalent in IVC. Relevantly, in Mesopotamia, the eye-patterned apotropaic gemstone-beads imported from IVC were called “fish-eye” stones (“NA4-IGI- ḪA”, “NA4-IGI-KU6”), and \\\"eye-stones\\\" of Meluhha (\\\"NA4-IGI-ME-LUḪ-ḪA\\\"), whereas in Dravidian languages, carnelian (“akki-kal”), tiger-eye-like sardonyx (“puli-k-kaṇ-kal”), and cat’s-eye-like chrysoberyl (“pūṉai-k-kaṇ-kal”) have eye-stone related epithets. Moreover, “maṇi”, the Indus word for gemstones, which remained fossilized in ancient documents of Near East, arguably has a Dravidian root. Intriguingly, “maṇi” has been signifying meanings like “amulet” (see Atharvaveda), “bead”, “apple of eye”, and “gemstone” in Indian languages, each which were linked to the amuletic, eye-patterned gemstone beads of Meluhah.</jats:p>\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.2139/ssrn.4412558\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2023-01-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.4412558\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"posted-content\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Elsevier BV', 'container_title': '', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '', 'issn': [], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'posted-content', 'member': '78', 'prefix': '10.2139'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1017/s0003598x00040709\",\n  \"title\": \"Dravidian and the Decipherment of the Indus Script\",\n  \"authors\": \"T. Burrow\",\n  \"abstract\": \"<jats:p>The Indus script has been known for a little over 40 years, and it has so far resisted all attempts at decipherment. This is not surprising in view of the fact that the inscriptions are all very short, and that no long connected texts are preserved. It is also not known what language lies behind the writing: bilinguals are nonexistent, and given the historical circumstances, not likely to be found. In the period since the discovery of the inscriptions a number of attempts at decipherment has been made, but none has carried conviction. Quite recently there has been a number of fresh attempts, of which the most sustained and ambitious, by a group of Finnish scholars, has been described in the previous issue of this journal. It is based on the assumption that the language spoken in the Indus cities was a form of Proto-Dravidian. The detailed methods used in this attempt at decipherment have been briefly described in the previous articles, and they need not be repeated here. Instead it is proposed to examine the handling of the Dravidian material, which, as will be seen, is open to considerable criticism</jats:p>\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1017/s0003598x00040709\",\n  \"published_date\": \"1969-12-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1017/s0003598x00040709\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 5,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Cambridge University Press (CUP)', 'container_title': 'Antiquity', 'volume': '43', 'issue': '172', 'page': '274-278', 'issn': ['0003-598X', '1745-1744'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '56', 'prefix': '10.1017'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1017/s0041977x00141837\",\n  \"title\": \"Asko Parpola and others: Decipherment of the Proto-Dravidian inscriptions of the Indus civilization: a first announcement.—Progress in the decipherment of the Proto-Dravidian Indus script.—Further progress in the Indus script decipherment. [All three by] <i>Asko Parpola, Seppo Koskenniemi, Simo Parpola and Pentti Aalto</i>. (Scandinavian Institute of Asian Studies. Special Publications, Nos. 1–3.) 72 pp.; 47 pp.; [i], 46 pp. Copenhagen: Scandinavian Institute of Asian Studies, 1969, 1969,1970.\",\n  \"authors\": \"J. R. Marr\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1017/s0041977x00141837\",\n  \"published_date\": \"1971-02-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1017/s0041977x00141837\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 2,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Cambridge University Press (CUP)', 'container_title': 'Bulletin of the School of Oriental and African Studies', 'volume': '34', 'issue': '1', 'page': '160-164', 'issn': ['0041-977X', '1474-0699'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '56', 'prefix': '10.1017'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.31235/osf.io/u3xmd\",\n  \"title\": \"Ancestral Dravidian Languages in Indus Civilization: Ultraconserved Dravidian Tooth-word Reveals Deep Linguistic Ancestry and Supports Genetics\",\n  \"authors\": \"Bahata Ansumali Mukhopadhyay\",\n  \"abstract\": \"<p>Ever since the discovery of Indus valley civilization, scholars have debated the linguistic identities of its people. This study analyzes numerous archaeological, linguistic, archaeogenetic and historical evidences to claim that the words used for elephant (like, ‘pīri’, ‘pīru’) in Bronze Age Mesopotamia, the elephant-word used in the Hurrian part of an Amarna letter of ca. 1400 BC, and the ivory-word (‘pîruš’) recorded in certain sixth century BC Old Persian documents, were all originally borrowed from ‘pīlu’, a Proto-Dravidian elephant-word, which was prevalent in the Indus valley civilization, and was etymologically related to the Proto-Dravidian tooth-word ‘*pal’ and its alternate forms (‘*pīl’/‘*piḷ’/‘*pel’). This paper argues that there is sufficient morphophonemic evidence of an ancient Dravidian ‘*piḷ’/‘*pīl’-based root, which meant ‘splitting/crushing’, and was semantically related to the meanings ‘tooth/tusk’. This paper further observes that ‘pīlu’ is among the most ancient and common phytonyms of the toothbrush tree Salvadora persica, which is a characteristic flora of Indus valley, and whose roots and twigs have been widely used as toothbrush in IVC regions since antiquity. This study claims that this phytonym ‘pīlu’ had also originated from the same Proto-Dravidian tooth-word, and argues that since IVC people had named their toothbrush trees and tuskers (elephants) using a Proto-Dravidian tooth-word, and since these names were widely used across IVC regions, a significant population of Indus valley civilization must have used that Proto-Dravidian tooth-word in their daily communication. Since ‘tooth’ belongs to the core non-borrowable ultraconserved vocabulary of a speech community, its corollary is that a significant population of IVC spoke certain ancestral Dravidian languages. Important insights from recent archaeogenetic studies regarding possible migration of Proto-Dravidian speakers from Indus valley to South India also corroborate the findings of this paper.</p>\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.31235/osf.io/u3xmd\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2021-01-31T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.31235/osf.io/u3xmd\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"posted-content\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Center for Open Science', 'container_title': '', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '', 'issn': [], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'posted-content', 'member': '15934', 'prefix': '10.31235'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1515/9783110800258.119\",\n  \"title\": \"PROSPECTS FOR THE LINGUISTIC INTERPRETATION OF THE PROTO-INDIAN TEXTS (ON THE BASIS OF THE DRAVIDIAN LANGUAGES)\",\n  \"authors\": \"Ν. V. GUROV\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1515/9783110800258.119\",\n  \"published_date\": \"1976-12-31T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1515/9783110800258.119\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"book-chapter\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'DE GRUYTER', 'container_title': 'The Soviet Decipherment of the Indus Valley Script', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '119-138', 'issn': [], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'book-chapter', 'member': '374', 'prefix': '10.1515'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1017/s0035869x00128461\",\n  \"title\": \"Decipherment of the proto-Dravidian inscriptions of the Indus civilization. A First Announcement. Progress in the decipherment of the proto-Dravidian Indus script. By Asko Parpola and others. (The Scandinavian Institute of Asian Studies Special Publications Nos. 1 and 2.) pp. 72 and 47. Copenhagen, 1969.\",\n  \"authors\": \"William C. Brice\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1017/s0035869x00128461\",\n  \"published_date\": \"1970-04-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1017/s0035869x00128461\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 1,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Cambridge University Press (CUP)', 'container_title': 'Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society of Great Britain &amp; Ireland', 'volume': '102', 'issue': '2', 'page': '221-223', 'issn': ['0035-869X', '2051-2066'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '56', 'prefix': '10.1017'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.2307/j.ctvzgb7m5.8\",\n  \"title\": \"The Buddhist Indus Script\",\n  \"authors\": \"\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.2307/j.ctvzgb7m5.8\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2020-03-26T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvzgb7m5.8\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"book-chapter\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Harrassowitz Verlag', 'container_title': 'The Buddhist Indus Script and Scriptures', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '9-64', 'issn': [], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'book-chapter', 'member': '1121', 'prefix': '10.2307'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.2139/ssrn.5079207\",\n  \"title\": \"Indus Script’s Gemstone and Precious Shiny Commodity Related Fish-Signs, and Indus Gemstone-Word “Maṇi”: Ancestral Dravidian Symbolism in Indus Logograms and Language(s)?\",\n  \"authors\": \"Bahata A. Mukhopadhyay\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.2139/ssrn.5079207\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2025-01-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.5079207\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"posted-content\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 1,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Elsevier BV', 'container_title': '', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '', 'issn': [], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'posted-content', 'member': '78', 'prefix': '10.2139'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.2307/j.ctvr43jmf.9\",\n  \"title\": \"The Indus Valley Script\",\n  \"authors\": \"\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.2307/j.ctvr43jmf.9\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2015-02-06T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvr43jmf.9\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"book-chapter\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Archaeopress Publishing Ltd', 'container_title': 'The Archaeology and Epigraphy of Indus Writing', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '4-12', 'issn': [], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'book-chapter', 'member': '1121', 'prefix': '10.2307'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.2139/ssrn.4900721\",\n  \"title\": \"Deciphering Indus script and Pashupati Seal of Indus Valley Civilization\",\n  \"authors\": \"Shahbaig Kahoor Khan\",\n  \"abstract\": \"<jats:p>Indus Valley Civilization is one of the left out archaeological treasures, that is being ignored in the recent times. This paper is a guide to the scholars and the archaeologist, which will help them to solve the mysteries of the ancient Indus Civilization and its Script. The paper includes thorough research observations on the interpretation of the scripts. Among the script and the famous Pashupati seal has also been thoroughly interpreted and explained. This could be a guide to interpret Indus customs and culture more nearer to the truth of past. The political controversies altering the history of Indus can cause a tremendous if not correctly study.</jats:p>\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.2139/ssrn.4900721\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2024-01-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.4900721\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"posted-content\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 1,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Elsevier BV', 'container_title': '', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '', 'issn': [], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'posted-content', 'member': '78', 'prefix': '10.2139'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.21275/sr251216111751\",\n  \"title\": \"Sanskrit Influence on the Indus Script\",\n  \"authors\": \"Jeyakumar Ramasami\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.21275/sr251216111751\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2025-12-20T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.21275/sr251216111751\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'International Journal of Science and Research', 'container_title': 'International Journal of Science and Research (IJSR)', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '1329-1334', 'issn': ['2319-7064'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '8810', 'prefix': '10.21275'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1093/acprof:oso/9780190226909.003.0005\",\n  \"title\": \"The Indus Religion and the Indus Script\",\n  \"authors\": \"Asko Parpola\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1093/acprof:oso/9780190226909.003.0005\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2015-09-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1093/acprof:oso/9780190226909.003.0005\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"book-chapter\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 1,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Oxford University Press', 'container_title': 'The Roots of Hinduism', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '25-32', 'issn': [], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'book-chapter', 'member': '286', 'prefix': '10.1093'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.2307/j.ctvr43jmf.13\",\n  \"title\": \"Numerals in the Indus Script and their Uses\",\n  \"authors\": \"\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.2307/j.ctvr43jmf.13\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2015-02-06T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvr43jmf.13\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"book-chapter\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Archaeopress Publishing Ltd', 'container_title': 'The Archaeology and Epigraphy of Indus Writing', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '66-76', 'issn': [], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'book-chapter', 'member': '1121', 'prefix': '10.2307'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.35543/osf.io/kacfr\",\n  \"title\": \"Initial Decipherment  of Indus  script\",\n  \"authors\": \"Ramkumar Unnipillai Santhakumari\",\n  \"abstract\": \"<p>A partial decipherment of Indus script is described. Sound values for Indus characters , readings obtained and conclusions derived are explained here. The Indus script seems derived from Protocuneiform script and in turn Brahmi script seems derived from the Indus script. The decipherment seems to indicate that the seals were a form of identification. There is evidence of an early form of caste system prevalent among Dalits in Southern India in these seals. The readings show that the language used was of Dravidian origin</p>\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.35543/osf.io/kacfr\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2020-03-15T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.35543/osf.io/kacfr\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"posted-content\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Center for Open Science', 'container_title': '', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '', 'issn': [], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'posted-content', 'member': '15934', 'prefix': '10.35543'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.31219/osf.io/aqey4\",\n  \"title\": \"Indus script Decipherment\",\n  \"authors\": \"Ramkumar Unnipillai Santhakumari\",\n  \"abstract\": \"<p>A  partial decipherment of the  Indus script is described here. Similarities between Protocuneiform characters and Indus script characters were observed. Transferring meanings  from Protocuneiform to Indus script characters using ProtoDravidian sounds  resulted in meaningful readings .  These meanings as well as relations observed between Indus script and Brahmi script are described here.</p>\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.31219/osf.io/aqey4\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2019-10-02T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.31219/osf.io/aqey4\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"posted-content\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Center for Open Science', 'container_title': '', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '', 'issn': [], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'posted-content', 'member': '15934', 'prefix': '10.31219'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.22541/au.172366258.87422803/v1\",\n  \"title\": \"THE INDUS SCRIPT AS AN ALPHABET\",\n  \"authors\": \"Mahaveer H Muhammad\",\n  \"abstract\": \"<jats:p>This paper presents a novel approach to decoding the Indus script, which was used by the Indus Valley Civilization (IVC) from approximately 3300 to 1900 BCE. The main focus of this research is the structural analysis of the signs within the Indus script. The central objective of the paper is to determine whether the Indus script can be classified as an alphabet, and it aims to provide compelling evidence regarding the number of primary signs. This includes establishing the actual count of Indus signs through the decomposition of compound signs, identifying diacritics and analyzing their consistent usage, as well as exploring the underlying concepts and implications associated with sign extraction. To comprehensively examine the Indus script, the paper analyzes over 400 signs, encompassing the entire script rather than a select subset of signs or texts. The visual decomposition of the signs employs a simple grid methodology. By summarizing the logical findings, the paper successfully identifies the primary signs and develops an understanding of their phonetic representation within the alphabet. The study recognizes the diverse writing styles used to combine the basic signs, The writing style of the Indus script incorporates variations in formation, composition, and combination, resulting in a significant number of signs. The research findings indicate that the Indus Script primarily consists of only 40 core signs.</jats:p>\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.22541/au.172366258.87422803/v1\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2024-08-14T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.22541/au.172366258.87422803/v1\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"posted-content\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Wiley', 'container_title': '', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '', 'issn': [], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'posted-content', 'member': '311', 'prefix': '10.22541'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.31219/osf.io/4rga5_v1\",\n  \"title\": \"Deciphering the Indus Script\",\n  \"authors\": \"Milind Raskar\",\n  \"abstract\": \"<p>AbstractDeciphering Indus script was long a challenge for experts because of the complex structure of its clusters of signs, its inconspicuous traces in both past known scripts and scripts currently used in the Indian subcontinent, and the paradigms about potential Indus languages. Purpose of this study, is to extract the Indus letters from the common graphemes of synonymous words from different known Indian languages, depicting the pictographic signs present on Indus seals using the concept of the contemporary Egyptian hieroglyphic writing system. Furthermore, this study determined the reading sequence of the signs present in the clusters of the Indus seals. According to the findings, the language used by the Indus people is Proto-Pali, which uses an Abugida-type writing system with five vowels and eighteen consonants. Clusters of the signs are conjunct consonants or compound words that use diacritic syllables similar to the existing Indian subcontinent scripts. These findings will help reveal the previously occluded history of the Indus people and their historical relationship with existing Indian scripts, languages and culture.</p>\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.31219/osf.io/4rga5_v1\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2022-08-04T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.31219/osf.io/4rga5_v1\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"posted-content\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Center for Open Science', 'container_title': '', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '', 'issn': [], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'posted-content', 'member': '15934', 'prefix': '10.31219'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.21275/sr251225140842\",\n  \"title\": \"The Influence of Egyptian Hieroglyphics on the Indus Script\",\n  \"authors\": \"Jeyakumar Ramasami\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.21275/sr251225140842\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2025-12-31T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.21275/sr251225140842\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'International Journal of Science and Research', 'container_title': 'International Journal of Science and Research (IJSR)', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '2134-2139', 'issn': ['2319-7064'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '8810', 'prefix': '10.21275'}\"\n}"
